      "math_academy_id": "e-5214",
      "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse $(x-2)^2 + \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14193836/assets/db1ffc11b3321e87f7c5d2cb490d0381f4b950dfcfd64be00f49c07577f797c0.png)",
